---
// Why NM's Different. Promoted from the our-approach-v2 draft after a
// content-redesign pass (see CONTENT_TRIM_LOG.md) -- this is the canonical
// "why we're different" page every other page is supposed to link to
// rather than re-derive, and it was repeating itself three times in three
// wrappers (a 5-panel stepper, a 6-card differences grid, and a Model
// section, all making the investigation-first / Sandia GEO points more
// than once). Cut the two duplicate diff-grid cards (six things down to
// four -- the four remaining are genuinely new material), trimmed the
// Model section's mechanism list down to the point it's actually making,
// and cut the closing "Land of Entrapment" dark band, which just re-said
// Section 1's own pull-quote with no new information. The 5-accordion
// stack from the original page is now a numbered stepper rail.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = "Why NM's Different — TLS Foundations";
const summary =
  "New Mexico's soil variation is extreme enough that a repair method chosen for one neighborhood can be the wrong choice two miles away -- which is the whole argument against a national playbook and the reason TLS was built around decades of regional field experience instead. Five accordions unpack what that means in practice: the ground's real variability, why regional knowledge can't be shortcut, why the same industry-standard tools produce different outcomes depending on when they're picked, the real difference between a contractor inspection and an independent geotechnical investigation, and an honest answer to whether teaching competitors this stuff is a problem (it isn't).";
const description =
  "New Mexico has more soil variation than most states. 33 counties, 16 geologic basins -- a repair approach built around that, not a national playbook.";

const serviceAreaText = `New Mexico has more geological variation than most states combined — and that variation determines everything about how foundation repair should be approached here. TLS Foundations was built by people who chose New Mexico, stayed in New Mexico, and have spent their working lives learning this specific place. Thirty-three counties. Sixteen geologic basins. One contractor who knows the difference between what the ground does in the South Valley versus Rio Rancho versus Corrales — and why that difference matters before a single pier gets specified. Foundation repair looks different when the contractor has spent decades in the same formations, across the same moisture cycles, in the same communities. The method follows the diagnosis. The recommendation follows the evidence. TLS Foundations was built around that sequence — investigation before recommendation, monitoring when repair is premature, drainage correction when drainage is the problem, and a straight answer when the foundation does not need repair at all. The six operational differences on this page are not marketing language. They are the reason an honest evaluation is possible here when it is not possible at companies built around sales volume. We fix foundations. We just make sure yours needs fixing first.`;

const accordionFaqs = [
  {
    q: 'New Mexico has more soil variation than most states -- how does that affect foundation repair?',
    a: "The Rio Grande Rift runs the length of the state, filling long basins with sediment ranging from expansive clays to collapsible desert silts to alluvial river gravels, sometimes within a few miles of each other. A repair method that works reliably in the South Valley may be the wrong choice in Rio Rancho two miles away. A national company operating from a standardized playbook cannot account for that variation -- the method gets selected before the soil is understood.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does regional geotechnical knowledge matter, and why can\'t it be onboarded?',
    a: "Regional geotechnical knowledge takes decades to build. It accumulates from working the same formations, in the same climate, across multiple construction eras and moisture cycles, until the site reads before the boring goes in. That kind of knowledge lives in the people who have done the work in this state, in these soils -- it isn't in a training manual and can't be acquired quickly.",
  },
  {
    q: "If the repair methods aren't proprietary, what makes TLS Foundations different?",
    a: 'The methods -- helical piers, push piers, micropiles, compaction grouting, foam injection -- are industry-standard and available to any contractor. The difference is not the tool. The difference is whether the tool is selected before or after the soil is understood. A company with one system evaluates every problem through that system; TLS carries a broad enough toolkit that the method genuinely follows the diagnosis.',
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between a contractor inspection and a geotechnical investigation?",
    a: "A contractor inspection is performed by someone whose business depends on repair sales, which creates an inherent conflict of interest even when the evaluation itself is accurate. A geotechnical investigation is a separate, formal step -- not every investigation covers the same ground, but depending on scope it can include a licensed engineer documenting soil conditions and recommending a course of action independent of any repair, with no financial stake in the outcome. It isn't part of a standard site visit. When the decision involves significant money, or you are not sure whether you need repair at all, that independent investigation is the more reliable starting point.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Are you worried competitors will use this website to get better at their jobs?',
    a: 'No. A rising tide lifts all boats -- if the content on this site makes any contractor, including a competitor, think more carefully about soil conditions before recommending a repair method, homeowners benefit. And the knowledge that makes TLS different is not in a document; it is in the people who have spent decades in New Mexico soils, which does not transfer by reading a page.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: accordionFaqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Our Approach', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/our-approach' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="full"
  schema={schema}
  ogImage="/images/our-approach-hero.webp"
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Our Approach"
  nextPage={{ path: '/why-foundations-move', name: 'Why Foundations Move' }}
  footerProps={{ serviceAreaLabel: "NM is different, but so are we.", serviceAreaText }}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <img class="hero-photo" src="/images/our-approach-hero.webp" alt="New Mexico high desert landscape" fetchpriority="high" />
    <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
    <div class="tls-hero-wrap">
      <div class="tls-hero-box">
        <h1 class="tls-hero-headline">Uniquely New Mexico.</h1>
        <span class="tls-hero-sub">(The ground. The people. The attitude.)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="tls-approach">
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <span class="eyebrow">The place</span>
        <h2 class="section-headline">New Mexico is not just a place. It's an attitude.</h2>
        <p class="body-p">The ground reflects that — unpredictable, independent, not interested in behaving the way people expect. <strong>Neither are most New Mexicans. Neither is the way we approach foundation work.</strong></p>
        <p class="body-p">TLS Foundations was built here, by people who chose to be here and spent their working lives learning this specific place — its geology, its climate, its construction history, its communities. That's not a marketing statement; you cannot fake 25 years of New Mexico field work, and it determines every evaluation we do, every recommendation we make, and every method we apply.</p>
        <div class="pull">
          <p>Thirty-three counties. Different soils, different structures, different green chile cheeseburger arguments. We know them all. This is the Land of Entrapment — and we are not leaving.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="stat-band">
      <div class="stat-grid">
        <div class="stat-item">
          <span class="stat-number">33</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Counties</span>
          <span class="stat-prose">Every jurisdiction, every soil province, every climate zone in the state</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-item">
          <span class="stat-number">16</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Geologic basins</span>
          <span class="stat-prose">Each with its own depositional history, its own clay mineralogy, its own way of moving</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-item">
          <span class="stat-number">2</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Owners</span>
          <span class="stat-prose">Who still answer the phone, still show up, still know what the ground does here</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Five accordions replaced with one numbered stepper rail. Anchor ids
         (#toolkit, #investigation, #rising-tide) preserved -- other pages
         link straight to these. -->
    <section class="stepper-block">
      <div class="stepper-inner">
        <div class="stepper-header">
          <span class="eyebrow">What this means in practice</span>
          <h2 class="section-headline">Five things worth understanding before you call anyone.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stepper-rail" role="tablist">
          <button class="step-btn active" data-step="ground" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
            <span class="step-num">01</span>
            <span class="step-label-group"><span class="step-label">The ground</span><span class="step-desc">Why NM's soil variation breaks a national playbook</span></span>
          </button>
          <button class="step-btn" data-step="knowledge" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
            <span class="step-num">02</span>
            <span class="step-label-group"><span class="step-label">The knowledge</span><span class="step-desc">Why regional experience can't be shortcut or onboarded</span></span>
          </button>
          <button class="step-btn" data-step="toolkit" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
            <span class="step-num">03</span>
            <span class="step-label-group"><span class="step-label">The toolkit</span><span class="step-desc">Same tools as everyone else — different order of operations</span></span>
          </button>
          <button class="step-btn" data-step="investigation" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
            <span class="step-num">04</span>
            <span class="step-label-group"><span class="step-label">The investigation</span><span class="step-desc">What a real geotechnical investigation actually is</span></span>
          </button>
          <button class="step-btn" data-step="rising-tide" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
            <span class="step-num">05</span>
            <span class="step-label-group"><span class="step-label">The competition</span><span class="step-desc">Does teaching competitors this worry us? No.</span></span>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="stepper-panels">
          <div class="step-panel active" id="ground" data-panel="ground">
            <h3 class="step-title">New Mexico has more soil variation than most states. That variation is not subtle.</h3>
            <p class="body-p">The Rio Grande Rift runs the length of the state — a zone where the earth's crust is actively pulling apart, filling long basins with thousands of feet of sediment. Those basin sediments range from <a href="/expansive-soil" class="body-link">expansive clays</a> to <a href="/collapsible-soil" class="body-link">collapsible desert silts</a> to alluvial river gravels, sometimes within a few miles of each other. The mountain ranges that frame the basins produce their own soil character at their margins. The high desert plains, the Colorado Plateau, the Permian formations of the southeast, the volcanic fields — each brings its own geological history and its own foundation implications.</p>
            <p class="body-p">What this means in practice: a repair method that works reliably in the South Valley may be the wrong choice in Rio Rancho two miles away. The soil under a foundation in Corrales — alluvial, moisture-influenced by centuries of acequia irrigation — behaves differently than the soil under a foundation in Tijeras, where bedrock proximity and elevation change the calculation entirely. Socorro sits in a seismically active corridor on Rio Grande alluvial deposits with specific liquefaction considerations that do not apply in Albuquerque's East Mesa neighborhoods. A standardized national playbook can't account for that — it selects the method before the soil is actually understood.</p>
            <p class="body-p"><a href="/why-foundations-move" class="body-link">See our full reference: Why Foundations Move in New Mexico →</a></p>
          </div>

          <div class="step-panel" id="knowledge" data-panel="knowledge">
            <h3 class="step-title">Regional geotechnical knowledge takes decades to build. It cannot be onboarded.</h3>
            <p class="body-p">Regional geotechnical knowledge takes decades to build. It accumulates from showing up in the same formations, in the same climate, across multiple construction eras and moisture cycles, until the site reads before the boring goes in. That kind of knowledge isn't in any training manual. It lives in the people who have done the work — in this state, in these soils, long enough for the ground to stop surprising them.</p>
            <p class="body-p">We work closely with geotechnical engineers and refer to them often. That relationship works because we speak the same language — not because we took a course, but because 25 years of New Mexico field work produces a shared frame of reference. When an engineer hands us an investigation report, we know what it means for the repair. When we refer a homeowner to an engineer, we know who to call for which formation.</p>
            <p class="body-p">That knowledge base — built across every region of the state and informed by geotechnical investigation through our affiliate <a href="https://www.sandiageo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="body-link">Sandia GEO</a> — was assembled here and remains here. In the people doing the work.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="step-panel" id="toolkit" data-panel="toolkit">
            <h3 class="step-title">We use the same tools as everyone else. We just don't sell them before we understand your building.</h3>
            <p class="body-p">The methods are not proprietary. Helical piers, push piers, micropiles, compaction grouting, foam injection — these are industry-standard approaches available to any contractor. The difference is not the tool. The difference is whether the tool is selected before or after the soil is understood.</p>
            <p class="body-p">A company with one system evaluates every problem through that system. We carry a broad enough toolkit that the method genuinely follows the diagnosis — and when no standard method fits, we design around the problem rather than force a fit.</p>
            <p class="body-p"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">See the full toolkit and how we decide →</a></p>
          </div>

          <div class="step-panel" id="investigation" data-panel="investigation">
            <h3 class="step-title">There's a real difference between a contractor inspection and a geotechnical investigation — and it's easy to go through the whole process without anyone explaining it.</h3>
            <p class="body-p">A contractor inspection is performed by someone whose business depends on repair sales. The evaluation is real — an experienced contractor can identify significant problems — but the business model creates real pressure on the recommendation (more on why, below).</p>
            <p class="body-p">A geotechnical investigation is a separate, formal step — not every investigation covers the same ground. Depending on scope, it can include a licensed engineer documenting soil conditions, identifying the mechanism driving movement, and recommending a course of action, independent of any repair and with no financial stake in what that action is. It isn't part of a standard site visit — it's something you can request when the decision involves significant money, or when you are not sure whether you need repair at all.</p>
            <p class="body-p">Our affiliate <a href="https://www.sandiageo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="body-link">Sandia GEO</a> provides geotechnical investigation separate from repair sales. We can also refer you to independent structural engineers when that level of analysis is warranted.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="step-panel" id="rising-tide" data-panel="rising-tide">
            <h3 class="step-title">Are we worried competitors will use this website to get better at their jobs?</h3>
            <p class="body-p">No. A rising tide lifts all boats. If the content on this site makes any contractor — including a competitor — think more carefully about soil conditions before recommending a repair method, homeowners benefit. That is the point.</p>
            <p class="body-p">The knowledge that makes TLS different is not in a document. It is in the people who have spent decades in New Mexico soils, building the kind of field library that cannot be read or downloaded. That does not transfer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stepper-panel-nav">
          <button class="step-nav-btn step-prev-btn" type="button" hidden>← <span class="step-nav-label"></span></button>
          <button class="step-nav-btn step-next-btn" type="button">Next: <span class="step-nav-label"></span> →</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="diff-section">
      <div class="diff-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">What this looks like in practice</span>
        <h2 class="section-headline">Four things that are structurally different about the way TLS works.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="diff-landscape">
        <div class="diff-card">
          <span class="diff-number">01</span>
          <h3>Drainage referred out honestly</h3>
          <p>When drainage correction is the right first step — and in New Mexico it often is — we say so and refer to contractors who do that work well. Starting with piers when drainage correction would suffice is not a service. It is a sale. If it's wet, keep it wet. If it's dry, keep it dry.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <span class="diff-number">02</span>
          <h3>Monitoring as a real recommendation</h3>
          <p>When the evidence does not support structural intervention, we recommend monitoring. A documented baseline, a return visit, a determination of whether movement is active or historic. That's a hard recommendation to make on commission. We can make it, because our evaluation doesn't depend on selling a repair.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <span class="diff-number">03</span>
          <h3>Honest about method limitations</h3>
          <p>Every repair method has conditions where it fails. We explain those conditions — including when helical piers are the wrong choice, when push piers won't work in light New Mexico residential construction, and when foam injection is being sold as something it is not.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <span class="diff-number">04</span>
          <h3>Custom solutions when standard methods don't fit</h3>
          <p>When the soil profile, structural conditions, or access constraints put a situation outside what standard systems address, we design around the problem. The goal is the right answer — not the nearest available product in the lineup.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="model-section">
      <div class="model-inner">
        <span class="eyebrow">The model</span>
        <h2 class="section-headline">Why this business works the way it does.</h2>
        <div class="model-lead">
          <p>We solve problems. We do not sell products. In New Mexico, that distinction matters more than anywhere else — because the problems here are genuinely different, and a product sold without understanding the problem is worse than no repair at all.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="body-p">Most foundation repair evaluations combine diagnosis and sales in the same visit, from the same person, often on commission. That's not dishonesty on any one company's part — it's just how the model is usually built, and it puts real pressure on every recommendation, whether the company means to or not.</p>
        <p class="body-p">TLS was built as the opposite. Two owners who came out of the industry, saw that structure up close, and built one where the person doing the evaluation has no stake in what gets sold afterward. Anti-sales pressure is not a positioning statement. It is how the business is designed.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="contact-close">
      <div class="contact-inner">
        <span class="contact-eyebrow">Ready when you are</span>
        <h2 class="contact-headline">We won't promise you a free estimate, but let's talk.</h2>
        <p class="contact-sub">Text or call — you reach the owner(s).</p>
        <div class="contact-options">
          <div class="contact-option">
            <span class="contact-option-label">Text</span>
            <a href="sms:5059914180" class="contact-option-value">Send a text →</a>
            <span class="contact-option-note">Usually same day</span>
          </div>
          <div class="contact-option">
            <span class="contact-option-label">Call</span>
            <a href="tel:5059914180" class="contact-option-value">(505) 991-4180</a>
            <span class="contact-option-note">The owner(s) answer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="contact-option">
            <span class="contact-option-label">Location</span>
            <span class="contact-option-value">Albuquerque,&nbsp;New Mexico</span>
            <span class="contact-option-note">The state is our office</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<script>
  function initStepper() {
    const rail = document.querySelector('.stepper-rail');
    if (!rail) return;
    const steps = Array.from(rail.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.step-btn'));
    const panels = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.step-panel'));
    const prevBtn = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.step-prev-btn');
    const nextBtn = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.step-next-btn');
    const stepLabel = (btn: HTMLButtonElement) => btn.querySelector('.step-label')?.textContent ?? '';

    function select(key: string, opts: { scroll?: boolean } = {}) {
      steps.forEach((s) => {
        const active = s.dataset.step === key;
        s.classList.toggle('active', active);
        s.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(active));
      });
      panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.toggle('active', p.dataset.panel === key));
      if (opts.scroll) {
        document.querySelector('.stepper-rail')?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
      }
      const index = steps.findIndex((s) => s.dataset.step === key);
      if (prevBtn) {
        prevBtn.hidden = index <= 0;
        const label = prevBtn.querySelector('.step-nav-label');
        if (label && index > 0) label.textContent = stepLabel(steps[index - 1]);
      }
      if (nextBtn) {
        nextBtn.hidden = index === -1 || index === steps.length - 1;
        const label = nextBtn.querySelector('.step-nav-label');
        if (label && index >= 0 && index < steps.length - 1) label.textContent = stepLabel(steps[index + 1]);
      }
    }

    steps.forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const key = btn.dataset.step;
        if (key) {
          select(key);
          history.replaceState(null, '', `#${key}`);
        }
      });
    });
    prevBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const current = steps.findIndex((s) => s.classList.contains('active'));
      if (current > 0) {
        const key = steps[current - 1].dataset.step;
        if (key) {
          select(key, { scroll: true });
          history.replaceState(null, '', `#${key}`);
        }
      }
    });
    nextBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const current = steps.findIndex((s) => s.classList.contains('active'));
      if (current >= 0 && current < steps.length - 1) {
        const key = steps[current + 1].dataset.step;
        if (key) {
          select(key, { scroll: true });
          history.replaceState(null, '', `#${key}`);
        }
      }
    });

    const initialId = window.location.hash.replace('#', '');
    if (steps.some((s) => s.dataset.step === initialId)) {
      select(initialId, { scroll: true });
    } else {
      select(steps[0]?.dataset.step ?? '');
    }
  }
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', initStepper);
</script>

<style>
